From 6950ca40444c5545ca2e72d3d1421c3549924f02 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 21:21:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 21/28] Arp: ratchet roll (ping-pong ball between closing
 plates) and bounce length

- Ratchet notes: new option Roll. The hits speed up like a ping-pong ball between two plates
  that meet where the ratchet ends: hit j at S (1 - r^j) of the ratchet's length S, r from the
  ratchet bounce (0.95 at +1 ... 0.5 at +10). Once the gaps would get shorter than 16 ms, the hits
  go on at 16 ms as a buzz until one such gap before the landing. Negative bounce plays the same
  hits backwards in time; bounce 0 is an even roll of 8 hits per step. Hits are computed as they
  come (no table), at most 255.
- Bounce length 1 ... 16: a ratchet can last several arp steps. It holds its note, its hits spread
  over all the steps, and the arp goes on with its next note on the step after them. The steps
  inside count for the rhythm and sequence length like rests. Synced, the clock's tick moves the
  ratchet into its next step; unsynced, render() does.
- Bounce fade becomes Bounce velocity: Even, Fade (as before), Rise (louder as the gaps shrink).
  Files keep 0/1 for the old off/on, 2 = Rise; the roll is stored as ratchetNotes 255 (older
  builds read it as 8).
- With bounce length 1 and no roll, everything is as before (same positions, velocities and
  file values).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
